Two files created:

---

### `ErrorBoundary.tsx`

**Key design decisions:**

| Feature | Detail |
|---|---|
| `getDerivedStateFromError` | Captures the error synchronously so the fallback renders immediately |
| `componentDidCatch` | Logs a structured object (`message`, `stack`, `componentStack`, `timestamp`) and calls the optional `onError` prop |
| `retry()` | Resets state to `null`, which causes React to re-render the children from scratch |
| `fallback` render prop | Lets callers supply their own UI while still receiving `error`, `errorInfo`, and `retry` |

**Props:**

```ts
interface Props {
  children: ReactNode;
  fallback?: (props: FallbackProps) => ReactNode;  // optional custom UI
  onError?:  (error: Error, info: ErrorInfo) => void; // e.g. send to Sentry
}
```

**Basic usage:**

```tsx
<ErrorBoundary onError={(e, info) => Sentry.captureException(e, { extra: info })}>
  <MyWidget />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

**Custom fallback:**

```tsx
<ErrorBoundary fallback={({ error, retry }) => (
  <div>
    <p>{error.message}</p>
    <button onClick={retry}>Retry</button>
  </div>
)}>
  <MyWidget />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

The default fallback UI includes an expandable stack-trace section so developers can inspect details without leaving the page. The example file (`ErrorBoundary.example.tsx`) demonstrates both patterns with a `<Bomb>` component that throws on demand.
